# GSEA POC Tiered-Execution Audit and Architecture Reconstruction

## 0. What this document is and how to task it later

This is the tree-grounded audit of using the GSEA POC (Grain-Stalk Execution
Architecture, D:/EmulatR/EmulatrPOC) as a second, fast execution tier inside
EmulatR V5. It answers the load-bearing unknowns in the Tiered Execution /
POC Injection Speculation Record by inspecting the actual POC source rather
than reasoning from memory.

Read the Speculation Record for the SHAPE of the plan and the gates. Read THIS
document for what the code actually is. Where the two conflict, this document
wins for facts; the Record still wins for strategy shape.

To task a future session from here:
- The confirmed / overturned ledger (Section 3) is the diff against the Record.
- The reframed gates (Section 5) replace GATE-1 and add two gates.
- The two-path execution design (Section 9) is the converged architecture; the
  scaffold that embodies it is systemLib/MachinePoc.h (placed 2026-07-22).
- The V5 seam facts (Section 10) are the verified CpuState / Machine anchors.
- The GATE-1 empirical probe + residency rule + reference ASM is Section 11.
- The deferred branches (Section 7) are the next inspection tasks.
- Source anchors (Section 8) are file:line for every claim.

## 1. One-paragraph verdict

The tiered-injection strategy is structurally sound and the POC is a much
stronger asset than the Record's cautious framing admits: a working Alpha
semantic core that reached SRM boot Phase 6 at a MEASURED ~90 M inst/sec
release (67-80 M sustained on real SRM execution), carrying its own
same-process validation harness. BUT three of the Record's load-bearing
technical premises are wrong -- the speed mechanism, the dispatch model, and
the abandonment narrative -- and correcting them changes what the make-or-break
gate should test. Proceed, but fix the model first.

## 2. What the POC actually is (reconstruction)

GSEA is a FLAT, FUNCTIONAL interpreter, not a staged pipeline. The journals
call it a "6-stage sequential pipeline," but in pipeline.h the six "stages"
are the six sequential STEPS of one loop iteration (fetch, encode, resolve,
execute, advance, interrupt-check). There are NO latches, NO per-cycle
occupancy / hazard state, NO BoxResult, and a 2-frame call stack (loop +
handler).

Execution model:
- Pipeline A (run_pipeline_a, pipeline.h:238): the main flat interpreter loop.
- Pipeline B (run_stalk, pipeline.h:468): a captured-loop replayer. A "stalk"
  is a pre-encoded loop body (<=32 grains of PL0 word + Handler pointer) that
  replays with no fetch/encode/resolve. This is the loop-acceleration lever
  (relates to WARP), measured at 63.7 M/s in a debug bench (9.7x over
  Pipeline A debug); the 300 M release figure is EST, not built.
- Dispatch: resolve_handler (handler_table.h:40) is a SINGLE flat switch on
  opcode that returns a Handler function pointer; the loop then does one
  indirect call. Header comment states verbatim: "Single flat switch -- no
  dispatcher functions, no two-level dispatch."
- Handlers: mostly C++ TEMPLATE signatures (signatures.h) instantiated per
  operation; comment claims "identical to hand-written at -O2, zero overhead,
  verified by benchmark." Plus direct handlers for irregular ops.
- State: a large ExecContext struct (exec_context.h:110) passed BY REFERENCE
  to every handler. Registers accessed via ctx.rf.ird()/iwr() indexing
  ctx.rf.ir[32] in memory. There is no locals-hoisting of guest state.
- Exceptions: faultCore_inl.h. raise_*() set EXC_ADDR, nextPC = PAL vector | 1,
  and exc_pending; the loop checks exc_pending after fetch and after execute
  and skips the normal advance. No pipeline latches to unwind -> precise by
  construction.
- Harness: RVF (Region Validation Framework). RegionDispatcher
  (region_dispatcher.h) wraps the production handler table and inserts
  RegisterDelta.snapshot_before/after/commit, IPRMonitor, InstructionAudit,
  and region-boundary snapshots. This is a same-process monitoring /
  differential precursor already in the tree.

Coverage that is real and debugged: full integer / logic / shift / byte / MVI /
multiply; memory with DTB lookup + VPTB walk; PAL (call_pal, hw_ld/st/mfpr/
mtpr/rei); FP IEEE and VAX. It reached SRM Phase 6, not a toy spike.

## 3. Confirmed vs overturned ledger (the diff against the Record)

CONFIRMED:
- Flat / functional / WARP-tier: the moment a handler computes an architectural
  result it leaves the cycle-accurate tier. Accurate (Record 1.3).
- The ~120x is REAL, not stale. Measured: 90 M inst/sec release; 67-80 M
  sustained on SRM execution. Against a slow tier of order ~0.75-1 M inst/sec
  that is ~90-120x. Discount the 300 M "stalk" and 600 M "JIT" rows -- both are
  EST in the Expected Platform Performance note; the JIT does not exist.
- Tiered injection over revert, with same-process differential and runtime
  fallback: sound design, and better supported than assumed (see RVF).

OVERTURNED (these matter):

O-1. Register-residency is NOT why it is fast, and GATE-1 tests the wrong thing.
  State is by-reference in a large ExecContext; there is no "materialize to
  locals across N instructions, sync at boundaries" -- not in Pipeline A, not
  in run_stalk (still passes ctx by reference, pipeline.h:477). The Record's
  mid-session "CORRECTION" (POC held context as locals, not by-ref) is itself
  wrong; the code is the by-reference structure the Record talked itself out of.
  What actually buys ~90x: absence of fidelity bookkeeping (no BoxResult
  populate/apply/trace, no stage advance, no hazard/occupancy), inline template
  handlers, batched IRQ checks (every 1024) and heartbeats (every 1M), inlined
  RAM access, flat 2-frame stack. CONSEQUENCE: GATE-1 as written ("confirm
  state stays register-resident") is the wrong probe -- the POC never did that
  and still hit 90M. The true make-or-break is state-representation sharing
  (see O-1 remedy in Section 5). EMPIRICALLY CONFIRMED in Section 11: with
  function-pointer dispatch the register file is memory-resident, reloaded
  every instruction across an indirect call.

O-2. There are NO family dispatchers. Dispatch is one flat switch returning a
  function pointer (handler_table.h:9,40). The Record's "per-family dispatchers
  distribute indirect-branch pressure = threaded dispatch achieved
  structurally" is not in the code. The POC has exactly the single shared
  indirect site the Record named as the #1 host-mispredict suspect (Record 2.2)
  and still hits 90M -- weak evidence AGAINST dispatch misprediction being the
  dominant slow-tier cost. "Table-driven generator that regenerates against V5
  conventions" is half-right: handlers are compile-time C++ templates (good,
  nothing to maintain); the resolve switch is hand-written. A grain generator
  lineage exists (the "620 grains" script; V5 genGrains.py) but it emits PL0
  metadata, not the dispatcher. So "edit the generator to fix state access
  across all handlers at once" does not apply as stated; edit the signature
  templates + accessor inlines instead (similar propagation).
  ADDENDUM (dispatch micro-opt): a precomputed function-pointer lookup table
  does NOT fix dispatch misprediction -- the mispredicting branch is the
  indirect CALL through the resolved pointer, identical whether resolve is a
  switch-jump-table or an array load. Threaded dispatch (computed-goto /
  musttail) reduces it, and that is clang/gcc-only, not MSVC. Real leverage:
  per-PC decoded-handler caching (amortize resolve to zero, which stalks
  already do) and, on the ARM64/clang Phase-5 target, tail-call threading.
  Do NOT refactor dispatch before TASK-PERF-1 confirms it is the top bucket.

O-3. The abandonment narrative is inaccurate on three counts.
  (a) Timing: last touched ~Apr-May 2026 (~2.5-3.5 months before 2026-07-22),
      not "6-8 months ago."
  (b) Cause: not an undiagnosable "exceptions with side effects" wall. The
      fault path is clean and functional; because there is no pipeline it is
      precise by construction, and stores gate side effects correctly (every
      store checks `if (pa == ~0ULL) return;` before writing, mem_ops.h). The
      journals show a normal, well-instrumented frontier: Phases 1-3 COMPLETE,
      Phase 5 console probe "UART poll breaks," Phase 6 reached, 9 TLB-insert
      bugs freshly fixed awaiting a clean-build verify run, and an ordered
      10-item next-session priority list. It was PAUSED (to concentrate on
      V4/V5, which reached P00>>>), not blocked.
  (c) "No Oracle then": partially true, but the POC already built RVF, the
      same-process differential/monitoring pattern the Record's Sections 5-6
      propose. It is a re-use, not a new build.

## 4. Feasibility and cost-benefit

Feasibility: YES, structurally, and the tiered design is the right shape. The
genuine cost centers, corrected for what is in the tree:

- State reconciliation (the real make-or-break). GSEA ExecContext (rf.ir/fr,
  iprs, tlb, shared_mem, palShadow, pal_mode as a bool derived from pc&1 at
  advance) vs V5 CpuState (separate palMode bool, BoxResult, pipeline latches).
  Because GSEA state is already a plain struct, this is a STRUCT-MAPPING
  problem, not a compiler-aliasing fight. More tractable than the Record
  implies, but it is the true effort sink. RESOLVED further by the V5 seam
  inspection (Section 10): the shared substrate is coreLib::CpuState itself,
  so the fast tier binds references and there is no separate mirror to map.
- FP is not bit-exact with the Oracle. GSEA FP uses host double/float and
  std::fesetround (signatures.h fop_t/fop_s/fop_t_rounded), NOT Berkeley
  SoftFloat, which V4 uses deliberately for bit-exactness (Record 1.1). The
  Oracle differential WILL flag every FP-bearing path. Decide up front: swap
  GSEA FP to SoftFloat, or class FP as a fidelity boundary that falls through
  to the slow tier. NOTE: V5 carries BOTH berkeley-softfloat-3-master AND an
  SSE-FP path (coreLib/alpha_SSE_fp_inl.h) -- the FP reconciliation may reduce
  to reusing whichever the Oracle commits through.
- Known fidelity holes (all match the provisional / C2-decode caution):
  arithmetic /V traps are STUBBED (`// TODO: queue arithmetic trap`,
  signatures.h -- overflow computed and result written, ARITH never raised);
  I_CTL SDE bit positions unverified; VPTB walk "was it ever active?";
  HW_LD/HW_ST DTB path unimplemented (journal priority list).

Benefit: a measured ~90x fast tier (bankable; ignore JIT/stalk estimates), a
working semantic core, and RVF (which discounts the harness cost budgeted in
Record 5-6). For OS bring-up wall-clock this is transformative, and every WARP
it lets you retire in favor of faithful execution is a fidelity GAIN (Record 7
fidelity ledger).

Cost / risk: a real multi-week subsystem port -- the handler regeneration
against CpuState, the FP decision, closing the stubbed traps, and full
re-verification against the V5 Oracle. The fast tier as-is is a functional
(WARP-tier) executor: correct as a validated fast path gated by the Oracle,
wrong if ever billed as the Oracle.

