## 9. Two-path execution design (converged 2026-07-22)

Decision: two execution PATHS over ONE shared state, dispatched at a coarse
boundary. Path 1 = V5 Classic (systemLib::Machine + PipelineDriver) -- the
faithful, cycle-accurate Oracle. Path 2 = MachinePoc -- the flat GSEA fast
tier. Both mutate the SAME coreLib::CpuState + memoryLib::GuestMemory, so a
tier handoff is a return of control, not a state copy.

Layered structure (three layers, not two headers):

        +-----------------------------+
        |   Orchestrator / tier gate  |  picks tier, owns --verify + region
        |   (future; slots at         |  allowlist + runtime dispatch flag +
        |    Machine::cpuKernel seam)  |  fallback to Classic on Defer/Fault
        +-----------------------------+
             |                    |
   +------------------+   +------------------+
   | ClassicTier      |   | MachinePoc       |   two executors, ONE state
   | (V5 faithful =   |   | (flat: Pipe A +  |   IExecTier::run(cpu,mem,budget)
   |  Oracle;         |   |  stalk Pipe B)   |   -> TierExit
   |  PipelineDriver) |   |  EMULATR_FAST_   |
   |                  |   |  TIER guarded    |
   +------------------+   +------------------+
             \                    /
          +-----------------------------+
          |  coreLib::CpuState (shared) |  intReg[32]/fpReg[32]/fpcr/
          |  + memoryLib::GuestMemory   |  intShadow[8]/pc/IPRs/halted
          +-----------------------------+

Contracts:
- ONE state, two executors. The shared substrate is CpuState itself (Section
  10). No separate POD/flat-vector mirror (proposed and rejected: it wins no
  addressing advantage over the already-contiguous regfile and re-introduces
  reconciliation and strict-aliasing/alignment hazards).
- COARSE boundary, never per-instruction virtual. IExecTier::run executes a
  tight internal loop and returns a TierExit reason: Continued / RegionExit /
  Defer / PalTransfer / Irq / Fault / Halted / MaxCycles. The IExecTier virtual
  fires per run() slice, not per grain. Terminal reasons map onto StopReason
  via toStopReason() for the post-mortem.
- Seam location. The natural insertion point is Machine::cpuKernel(CpuState&)
  (= PipelineDriver::step, the per-CPU action already factored out of the run
  loop; systemTick stays system-owned). The orchestrator chooses, per region,
  whether cpuKernel runs the Classic driver or MachinePoc.
- Oracle stays authoritative; poc adapts to CpuState. The fast tier only runs
  where the Oracle is GREEN -- enforced by MachinePoc::allowRegion(lo,hi), NOT
  a global switch. Secondary boot is off-limits until the slow tier boots it.
- --verify is the payoff. Same-state execution makes a divergence provably the
  tier difference. First cut diffs the architectural projection (regs, pc,
  IPRs, memory write-set) at the boot milestones Snapshot already captures
  (coarse-to-fine), which sidesteps per-region rollback.
- Guard discipline, twice. EMULATR_FAST_TIER (CMake compile guard, OFF by
  default) gates real execution; a runtime dispatch flag on the orchestrator
  gates which tier runs. MachinePoc.h compiles UNCONDITIONALLY (cannot
  bit-rot) with the real loop stubbed to TierExit::Defer -- same posture as the
  EMULATR_EV6_BPRED move.

Scaffold placed 2026-07-22: emulatrappuniv5/systemLib/MachinePoc.h
(namespace systemLib; include guard SYSTEMLIB_MACHINEPOC_H; PascalCase to match
Machine.h). Defines TierExit + tierExitName + operator<< + toStopReason,
