## 10. V5 execution-seam facts (verified 2026-07-22)

coreLib::CpuState (coreLib/CpuState.h) -- the shared substrate:
- uint64_t intReg[32], fpReg[32], fpcr, intShadow[8] (PAL shadow R4-7/R20-23).
- uint64_t pc (architectural PC). IPRs are FLAT members (cpu.ptbr, cpu.palBase,
  ...), canonical Alpha names, direct member access, NO accessor methods.
- bool halted (set at WB on BoxResult::faultCode == kFaultHalt).
- Contract: no leaf mutates CpuState directly; architectural writes go through
  BoxResult::regWriteValue/regWriteIdx applied at MEM; IPR writes via the
  CpuState* on ExecCtx are the documented exception. (This is the Classic path
  contract; MachinePoc deliberately writes CpuState directly -- that is the
  tier difference, and why it is not the Oracle.)

systemLib::Machine (systemLib/Machine.h) -- the Oracle orchestrator:
- run(maxCycles) -> systemLib::StopReason. step() -> bool (one PipelineDriver
  tick). stepCycle(i) -> bool (full per-cycle body).
- SPLIT: cpuKernel(CpuState&) = per-CPU action (PipelineDriver::step);
  systemTick(i) = once-per-quantum system bookkeeping (sentinel, snapshots,
  device-IRQ eval, IDLEWARP, interval-timer, b_irq diverts). This split is the
  tier-seam insertion point.
- Owns m_cpu (CpuState) + memory via m_chipset.guestMemory(); accessors cpu(),
  memory(), chipset(), mmio(). Implements pipelineLib::IFetchOverride
  (tryFetch/onBeforeFetch) for the SRM decompressor self-overwrite window --
  MachinePoc's fetch must honor the same override.
- applySpinSkip(SpinSkip::Plan) exists (systemLib/SpinSkip.h) -- the WARP/skip
  lever is already in the Oracle; census loop lengths at fast-tier speed before
  retiring any WARP (Record 7).

systemLib::StopReason (systemLib/StopReason.h): HaltedClean, OpcDecFault,
UnalignedFault, AcvFault, MemFault, MaxCyclesExceeded. Named stopReasonName()
(not toString) to avoid the doctest ADL clash -- follow the same rule for
tierExitName().

FP substrate: berkeley-softfloat-3-master AND coreLib/alpha_SSE_fp_inl.h both
present. GATE-FP reduces to reusing whichever the Oracle commits FP through.

## 11. GATE-1 empirical probe -- inlinable dispatch is the residency lever

Date: 2026-07-22. Status: INDICATIVE cross-compiler model, NOT the dispositive
gate. MSVC on the real handlers is the system of record (recipe below).

Method: compiled a CpuState-shaped POD + two GSEA-style handlers (h_addq,
h_ldq) + two dispatch loops with g++ 13.3.0 and clang 18.1.3 at -O2 (System V
ABI, Linux). Guest RAM marked __restrict (a distinct allocation from CpuState,
the real design intent). This proves the PATTERN, not the real handlers.
  - LoopA = opaque function-pointer dispatch (GSEA Pipeline A shape):
    resolve(raw) -> Handler pointer; call it.
  - LoopB = inlined direct dispatch (stalk / superinstruction shape): switch on
    opcode calling known handlers the compiler can inline.

Findings (g++ and clang agree):
- The CpuState base pointer is register-resident in BOTH loops (rdi / r13 /
  r15); fields are reached base+displacement: pc at [base+0x100], halted at
  [base+0x188], intReg[i] at [base + i*8]. "State as base+displacement from a
  hot pointer": CONFIRMED, and it is free (a struct member already is this).
- LoopA: exactly ONE indirect `call` per instruction; pc and the register file
  are round-tripped to MEMORY every instruction (reload `mov rax,[base+0x100]`;
  the handler writes back inside the call). State is NOT register-resident
  across instructions -- the opaque call forbids it; the hot base pointer is all
  you get. This empirically confirms O-1: the 90M did not come from residency.
- LoopB: ZERO calls; operands held in registers within the instruction; regfile
  writes are direct indexed stores (`mov [base + rc*8], result`). This is where
  residency appears. Hoisting pc into a loop-carried register (true stalk replay
  with a known body length) removes even the per-iteration pc reload.

RECOMMENDATION (the residency rule):
  GATE-1 passes to the exact degree the fast tier's dispatch is INLINABLE /
  visible to the loop. KEEP HANDLER BODIES VISIBLE -- stalks (Pipeline B),
  superinstructions, or templated direct dispatch. ANY routing through an opaque
  function pointer lands in LoopA: an indirect call per instruction with the
  register file in memory, REGARDLESS of how state is laid out. Corollary (ties
  to O-2): a precomputed function-pointer lookup table IS LoopA -- the wrong
  lever for residency. Layout choices (flat vector, POD, enum-indexed IPRs) do
  NOT change this; only dispatch visibility does. Design the fast tier so the
  hot loop sees its handler bodies.

What the model canNOT settle (needs the real build):
- MSVC is dispositive. On the Z640: `cl /O2 /FAsc machine_poc_probe.cpp`
  (interleaved source+asm) or `dumpbin /disasm MachinePoc.obj`, or a
  release-build Disassembly window at a loop breakpoint. Two compilers already
  agree, so MSVC agreeing is the strong prior. NOTE: this is a COMPILER listing;
  the linker map only gives symbol layout/sizes (a useful secondary signal for
  "did the handler inline away / did BoxResult symbols get pulled in").
- The model has NO per-cycle bookkeeping by construction, so it cannot test the
  OTHER half of GATE-1: that V5's BoxResult populate/apply/trace and pipeline
  machinery do not leak into the fast loop. That check only means anything
  against the real regenerated handlers bound to the real CpuState.

Reference ASM (g++ 13.3.0, -O2, Intel syntax; jump targets abbreviated to
<loopX+off>; `;` comments are annotations; CpuState offsets pc=+0x100,
halted=+0x188, intReg[i]=+i*8):

  --- LoopA: opaque function-pointer dispatch (Pipeline A shape) ---
  loopA:
    push  r15 ... push rbx            ; prologue
    mov   rbx,rcx                     ; n
    mov   r15,rdi                     ; r15 = CpuState* (HOT across loop)
    mov   rbp,rdx                     ; code base
    lea   r14,[rip+..]  # h_ldq       ; handler pointers precomputed
    lea   r13,[rip+..]  # h_addq
  .body:
    mov   rax,[r15+0x100]             ; RELOAD pc from memory
    mov   rdx,[rsp+0x8]               ; ram
    mov   rsi,r15                     ; &cpu arg
    and   eax,0x3fffc
    mov   edi,[rax+rbp*1]             ; fetch code word
    shr   eax,0x1a                    ; opcode
    cmp   eax,0x29
    mov   rax,r13
    cmove rax,r14                     ; select handler
    add   r12,0x1
    call  rax                         ; <== INDIRECT DISPATCH, 1 per instruction
    cmp   rbx,r12
    je    <loopA+0x7e>
    cmp   BYTE PTR [r15+0x188],0x0    ; RELOAD halted from memory
    je    .body
    ... epilogue ... ret
  Note: pc + regfile live in MEMORY; only r15 (base ptr) stays in a register.

  --- LoopB: inlined direct dispatch (stalk / superinstruction shape) ---
  loopB:
    mov   r9,rcx                      ; n
    cmp   BYTE PTR [rdi+0x188],0x0    ; halted (rdi = CpuState*, HOT)
    mov   r8,rdx                      ; code base
  .iter:
    mov   r10,[rdi+0x100]             ; pc into r10 for this iteration
    mov   rax,r10
    add   r10,0x4                     ; pc+4 held in register
    and   eax,0x3fffc
    mov   eax,[rax+r8*1]              ; fetch code word
    mov   ebx,eax
    shr   ebx,0x1a                    ; opcode
    ... decode ra/rb ...
    mov   r11,[rdi+rdx*8]             ; regfile read (rb) -> r11
    cmp   ebx,0x10
    jne   .ldq
    add   r11,[rdi+rdx*8]             ; addq: + regfile read (ra)  [held in reg]
    mov   [rdi+rax*8],r11             ; regfile WRITE, direct indexed store
    mov   [rdi+0x100],r10             ; pc writeback
    cmp   r9,rcx
    jne   .iter
    ... ret
  .ldq:
    movsx rax,ax                      ; disp
    add   rax,r11
    shr   rax,0x3
    mov   rax,[rsi+rax*8]             ; ram load (rsi = ram, __restrict)
    mov   [rdi+rdx*8],rax             ; regfile write, direct indexed
    mov   [rdi+0x100],r10             ; pc writeback
    ... loop
  Note: NO call; operands in registers; regfile writes are [base + idx*8].

